# glass.local.conf -- this machine's own glass levels. NOT tracked.
#
#   cp config/noctalia/glass.local.conf.example ~/.config/noctalia/glass.local.conf
#
# Copy it into place, edit it, and it applies here and nowhere else. Absent by
# default, which means "use the tracked levels in glass.conf" -- so a machine
# without this file needs nothing done to it.
#
# Why this exists
# ---------------
# glass.conf is tracked, so a level set there is a level set on every machine
# that pulls. But the right level is not a property of the setup, it is a
# property of the screen you are sitting in front of: a glass level that reads
# well on a bright panel at home is one you fight all day on a work laptop
# under office light, and neither answer is the one to commit for both.
#
# Same arrangement as ~/.config/hypr/host.lua, and for the same reason -- these
# are the two files that are allowed to differ per machine. Anything else that
# differs is a difference worth committing.
#
# How it resolves
# ---------------
# bin/noct-glass reads this file BEFORE glass.conf, and both of its keys before
# either of glass.conf's:
#
#   1. <scheme>.<key> here
#   2. <key> here
#   3. <scheme>.<key> in glass.conf
#   4. <key> in glass.conf
#   5. the built-in default
#
# The order matters at step 2 against step 3: a bare `window` set here beats a
# per-scheme `window` in glass.conf. Otherwise a machine that had said "no
# compositor transparency here" would get it back the moment you switched to the
# one scheme that overrides it.
#
# `noct-glass show` prints [glass.local.conf] when this file is in play, so
# "why do the two machines look different" has an answer you can read off.
#
# Every key glass.conf accepts works here, levels and blur alike. Nothing is
# inherited selectively -- a key you leave out simply falls through to glass.conf.

# --- what you probably want it for -----------------------------------------

# No translucency at all on this machine, terminal included.
# terminal = 1.0

# The other direction: the old look back, glass on every window. Costs contrast
# on every app that cannot fade its background without fading its text, which is
# all of them except kitty -- see glass.conf.
# window   = 0.85
# terminal = 0.55

# A dimmer backdrop, for a screen where the wallpaper reads too bright through
# a terminal.
# blur_brightness = 0.75
